BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 676.0 Tm (NTFS was introduced with Windows NT 3.1 in 1993, replacing the DOS-era FAT limitations for) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 659.0 Tm (a security-oriented, multiuser 32-bit operating system. It was designed for large volumes,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 642.0 Tm (robust metadata management, access control, and extensible file representation. The New) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 625.0 Tm (Technology lineage and its attribute-oriented record structures are central to) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 608.0 Tm (understanding NTFS's design.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 578.0 Tm (Later Windows releases evolved NTFS through additional attributes, indexes, reparse) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 561.0 Tm (points, compression, encryption, change journals and transactional support. Third-party) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 544.0 Tm (access tools and Linux's different NTFS drivers have varied in capability, but the on-disk) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 527.0 Tm (format itself remains an independent Microsoft-defined system. A reader must distinguish) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 510.0 Tm (volume revision and supported features, not assume identical semantics in every NTFS) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 493.0 Tm (implementation.) Tj ET
